if there's one thing that can get a  company absolutely destroyed on the  internet it's investing into nfts and  crypto riot games had to practically beg  for forgiveness and so did dead by  daylight people just hate the blockchain  despite all this one company that has  managed to not only rebrand itself using  the blockchain and still be able to  expand is facebook now named meta meta  ceo mark zuckerberg recently announced  that they were expanding their high tech  menlo park campus in silicon valley the  campus was already impressive to begin  with and so here's your exclusive look  into meta's massive headquarters  location  facebook or meta's headquarters lies in  menlo park on the edge of san francisco  bay spread out over 250 acres with over  30 buildings the entire complex includes  everything the employees might need to  get their creative juices flowing it's  more of a town instead of a tech company  campus the headquarters is a 10-minute  drive from the menlo park caltrain  station and regular shuttles are also  part of facebook's plan to help their  employees get to work more easily the  campus has tons of parking space too if  the employees want to drive to work  however employees have also been  allotted a transportation stipend so  they can take advantage of the public  transport system the team  more than 12 000 employees almost a  quarter of meta's entire global  workforce work at the menlo park meta hq  the team at menlo park is responsible  for a bit of everything engineering  communications and most importantly  virtual reality are all departments that  the menlo park team is actively focusing  on the virtual reality department  recently got a very big boost after  meta's metaverse announcement and is now  one of the most active departments at  the company the departments are arranged  pretty randomly so employees have to  pass different ones thereby increasing  chances of socialization apple does a  similar thing which they say helps give  rise to new ideas and promotes  innovation speaking about innovation  facebook has internal hackathons where  employees have to work on projects that  they don't normally work on this culture  dates back to when facebook was just  started and mark zuckerberg was hiring  and promoting employees based on the  winners of hackathons pretty cool huh  these hackathons are what resulted in  the development of the news feed feature  which now exists across pretty much all  social media platforms facebook might  have grown into a multi-billion dollar  company but they haven't yet let go of  the out-of-the-box approach that got  them there for a company named facebook  they're really not doing things by the  book i mean it's meta now but you get  the point unlike traditional  corporations that have gray lifeless  cubicles meta lets its workers customize  their workspaces and offices as much as  they want an employee of the company was  reported saying we try to hack our  spaces and no that does not mean that  they are hacking their computers that's  the job of the fbi it means things like  personalizing their desk by hanging a  pride flag from a banister or even going  as far as to add a full arcade in their  closets sounds a bit far-fetched well  what happened is that one of the  employees decided he wanted to play  games during his free time and brought a  japanese game on campus one day this  game was installed in his closet yep you  heard right other employees thought this  was a great idea and before you know it  the entire closet was a makeshift arcade  one guy even had a dance dance  revolution machine installed although  i'm not really sure how that would fit  in a closet what i'm trying to say here  is that facebook or well meta gives its  employees the power to do what they feel  would be helpful to them this lack of  micromanagement gives more control to  the employees and shows them that their  management trusts them now i'm going to  guide you through some of the other  highlights of the meta menlo park campus  so buckle up 1. main street  facebook decided to ditch the bench and  eye candy amusement park approach that  other companies used to swag up their  campuses instead facebook decided to  take a bit of inspiration from the  nearby palo alto main street and come up  with a main street of their own this  main street houses several cafes  eateries and even a health and wellness  center in case someone needs to go to  the doctor or grab some meds the main  street is also where most of the  employees hang out between meetings and  hang out with their colleagues you know  how some of the best ideas you get are  while eating good food well that's  exactly what facebook is banking on this  main street also makes the entire campus  look more like a small town rather than  a conventional corporate office it is  also what led to the start of open space  offices and tech companies now i've got  a cool little fact for y'all facebook  was the largest open space styled office  at the time of its new office's  commencement now let's go to  2 rooftop park  the mpk 20 has been embellished with the  additional feature of a 12.6 acre  rooftop park in zuckerberg's own words  frank gehry designed a palette of colors  and bands across the roof the lush lofty  park sprawls over the entire roof and  it's a soothing reprieve for employees  who often jog through the greenery  during their much needed blue light  break some of the best features of this  elevated garden include 90 native fauna  like the resident and migrant birds  hundreds of desedicious and flowering  trees over 150 thousand native shrubs  and a one-half mile long gravel pathway  not only that but the rooftop park also  has been tastefully adorned with gary  design tepees beautiful art  installations and swanky lawn furniture  lastly the mpk's rooftop cultivates a  strong adherence to environmental  sustainability through the installation  of solar panels these solar panels can  generate almost 2 million kilowatts of  electricity each year which is more than  enough to power 70 000 homes per day if  that's not impressive i don't know what  is metta actively encourages its  employees to go for walks on the rooftop  and has retrofitted the rooftop with  whiteboards and wi-fi to further boost  employee productivity research has  proven that walks surrounded by nature  boost creativity and apparently mark  zuckerberg himself uses this tactic to  get ideas he has given several  interviews on the rooftop and often  takes a walk there  3. freebies  who doesn't love freebies zuck knows  just how much people love free stuff and  the menlo park campus has tons of them  there's a free arcade system which has  now been fully leveled up with top of  the line gaming equipment and virtual  reality headsets food is also free  throughout the campus and last but  definitely not least is the free laundry  service meta has partnered with purple  thai to offer its employees free pickup  and delivery laundry service this  according to chloe myers a senior meta  employee is to make people's lives  easier the real treat however is the  culinary selection the campus offers  food from all over the world  remember how when men in black agent j  and k go for pie and it helps them save  the world well that's just the power of  good food in a relaxing environment  something which the aromatic biryani  scent you can find near the menlo park  cafeteria is trying very hard to prove  the menlo park campus with all its  impressive qualities would seem  incomplete without the addition of good  food to make sure this doesn't happen  metta has added culinary options from  all over the world in their campus from  simple stuff like a good cup of joe to  ethiopian and mexican delicacies meta  has tried to be as meta as possible to  make sure the environment is as good as  the food is it is rumored that the chefs  play ethnic cultural music matching the  locality of the food they're serving  that day that's not all much like their  strict adherence to privacy laws meta  has adhered to the rule of always giving  their best at least when it comes to  food art museum  frank gehry was involved in designing  the buildings of metta's menlo park  campus and was in one of these buildings  their in-house artist-in-residence  program led by guggenheim alum tina vaz  this program has placed 15 art  installations within the building the  most famous of these installations  consists entirely of recycled computer  parts like key caps and motherboard  fragments the artwork has been made to  look like tree roots and is inspired  from california's redwood forests leave  a thumbs up if you enjoyed the video  also a sub to the channel would be  monumental  see you in the next one
 

